Q: How to detect from Java if Python is installed A: <p>The implementation of the <code>array</code> commands depends on each <em>OS</em> (the example is valid in <em>Kubuntu</em>):</p>
<pre><code>void lanzarTerminalAndObtenerSuSalida() {
   try {
        // these are the commands to execute, listed:
      String commands[] = { &quot;python3&quot;, &quot;--version&quot; }; 

        // create the process using the following commands  
      Process process = new ProcessBuilder( commands ).start(); 

        // read the output 
      InputStream is = process.getInputStream();
      InputStreamReader isr = new InputStreamReader( is );
      BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader( isr );

        // as Abra suggests, since &quot;Java 17&quot;, you can
        // replace the previous three lines with:
        // BufferedReader br2 = process.inputReader();

      String line;
      while( ( line = br.readLine() ) != null ) {

           // here I do what I need with the &quot;answer&quot;
         System.out.println( line );
      }
      br.close();
      isr.close();
      is.close();
   }
   catch( IOException ex ) {   }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that each command goes as an individual item.</p>

Q: Hugo: Automatically Link Headers A: <p>This doesn't seem to be possible out of the box. There are two ways of working around it that I can think of: using JavaScript, as revelt suggested, or using HTML in your markdown.</p>
<p>For example, consider the HTML you supplied above.</p>

<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;a href=&quot;/post/cool-blog-post#some-interesting-title&quot;&gt;
    &lt;h2 id=&quot;some-interesting-title&quot;&gt;Some Interesting Title&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;/a&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>If you put this code directly into a Hugo markdown document, it will produce the kind of link you are looking for. However, this is a pain to type every time, so to reduce your work you can make a <a href="https://gohugo.io/extras/shortcodes/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">shortcode</a>.</p>
<p>In <code>layouts/shortcodes/link-heading.html</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>{{ $id := .Get 0 | lower | replaceRE &quot;[^0-9a-z]&quot; &quot;-&quot; | replaceRE &quot;-+&quot; &quot;-&quot; -}}
&lt;a href=&quot;#{{ $id }}&quot;&gt;
  &lt;h2 id=&quot;{{ $id }}&quot;&gt;{{ .Get 0 }}&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;/a&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>In your markdown document:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>{{&lt; link-heading &quot;Some Interesting Title&quot; &gt;}}
</code></pre>
<p>I have left the base URL out here, but you can pass it as a parameter from your markdown document if you want. (Of course, then you have to know what the URL is without having Hugo do it for you, which is not ideal.)</p>
<p>This approach has the disadvantages that you can't use the normal markdown heading syntax, and that you don't get Hugo's <a href="https://gohugo.io/extras/crossreferences/#what-about-duplicate-heading-anchors" rel="nofollow noreferrer">built-in resolution of duplicate anchors</a>. But it will get the job done.</p>

Q: Find key, value pair within a Python dict A: <p>If you want to check that a key contains a <em>specific</em> value (as it seems) then you need to 1) check to make sure the key is in the dictionary and if so, 2) check to make sure that the key's value is what you expect it to be. These must be done in that order since the second test will throw an error if the first step doesn't hold.</p>
<pre><code>k = &quot;Top&quot;
v = &quot;/Project3/Start&quot;

if k in entriesTLookup:
    if v in entriesTLookup[k]:
        print(&quot;found&quot;)
    else:
        print(&quot;not found&quot;)
else:
    print(&quot;not found&quot;)
</code></pre>
<p>Now this is a bit inelegant because there are two ways for these tests to fail, and we have to handle both of them (hence the two &quot;not found&quot; <code>print</code> calls). Fortunately we can combine them into one.</p>
<p>Because <code>and</code> short-circuits in Python (that is, the right half of the expression is never evaluated if the left half is <code>False</code>, because if the left half is <code>False</code> then the whole expression is <code>False</code> regardless of what the right half is) you can do this in one <code>if</code>. If either condition is <code>False</code> then the whole thing is <code>False</code> and flow moves to the <code>else</code> clause.</p>
<pre><code>k = &quot;Top&quot;
v = &quot;/Project3/Start&quot;

if k in entriesTLookup and v in entriesTLookup[k]:
    print(&quot;found&quot;)
else:
    print(&quot;not found&quot;)
</code></pre>
<p>You can also do it by using the dictonary's <code>get</code> method. This allows you to return a default value when the key is not found in the dictionary instead of raising a <code>KeyError</code>. Make sure that default value can't contain the target value and you're set. Here we're using an empty tuple, which will never contain any value you care to check for.</p>
<pre><code>k = &quot;Top&quot;
v = &quot;/Project3/Start&quot;

if v in entriesTLookup.get(k, ()):
    print(&quot;found&quot;)
else:
    print(&quot;not found&quot;)
</code></pre>

Q: Close modal on click outside or on the X icon A: <p>You already made the modal open from the URL, but what’s missing is telling it how to close.</p>
<p>Think of it like this:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>you told the modal: <em>“open when hash = #modal-faqs”</em></p>
</li>
<li><p>but you never told it: <em>“close when user clicks x or outside”</em></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>so when  user clicks the X, you need to hide the modal, and when user clicks outside, also hide the modal.</p>
<pre><code>document.addEventListener(&quot;DOMContentLoaded&quot;, function () {
const modal = document.getElementById('modal-faqs');
const closeBtn = modal.querySelector('.modal-close');

// open modal from URL
if (window.location.hash === '#modal-faqs') {
modal.style.display = 'block';
}

// close when clicking X
closeBtn.onclick = function () {
modal.style.display = 'none';
location.hash = ''; // remove hash
};

// close when clicking outside modal content
window.onclick = function (e) {
if (e.target === modal) {
modal.style.display = 'none';
location.hash = '';
        }
    };
});
</code></pre>
